    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "You should pivot your language from \"encroachment\" to terms like \"historical utility alignment\" or \"legacy infrastructure integration,\" which sound like administrative necessities rather than aggressive takeovers. Instead of framing it as a violation of territory, describe the situation as a \"pre-existing topological constraint\" that requires a \"technical overlay\" to function within the new mixed-use framework. This shifts the narrative from a hostile intrusion to a necessary engineering adjustment, much like a city trying to fit a new subway line into an old, slightly crooked tunnel without collapsing the whole thing.\n\nWhen they hear \"encroachment,\" they might imagine a hostile takeover, so try using phrases like \"lateral displacement of easement boundaries\" or \"non-intrusive utility realignment.\" This sounds bureaucratic and precise, like a spreadsheet formula adjusting a cell value, rather than a physical invasion. You can also frame the encroachment as a \"legacy artifact\" that the new zoning plan is simply \"digitizing\" or \"codifying\" rather than breaking.\n\nThink of it like a game of chess where the pieces have been moved slightly off the board for decades; you aren't trying to capture the king, you're just trying to make a legal move within",
